{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_245","type":"reasoning","text":"The files have one stray CRLF (probably at edges due to Set-Content writing CRLF for the heredoc lines; actually only 1 crlf each - odd). Actually PowerShell Set-Content with a here-string - the here-string contains LF line endings? It wrote CRLF presumably... but crlf=1 and lf=611 suggests only 1 CR. Hmm, probably the Set-Content normalized to CRLF... no, lf=611 and crlf=1 means mostly LF. Odd but the hash is over actual bytes, so fine.\n\nWait, that's weird. Let me not worry - the digest is computed over actual bytes, that's what matters, and SHA256SUMS will match.\n\nActually, one stray CRLF might come from the here-string's leading/trailing newline. Let me just normalize to LF to be cleaner and match repo conventions. Actually the repo uses -text pins only for specific files; normal files may be CRLF or LF. Let's normalize to LF for consistency.\n\nLet me normalize all my new files to LF."}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_246","type":"agent_message","text":"Let me normalize line endings to LF for consistency, then compute final hashes."}}
